ou can check this for  yourself but this works for whatever  round you finish up on you bet your  total losses so far plus 2 so whenever  you play this game as long as you go  through until you eventually  when around you always make two but this  seems like a contradiction you're not  supposed to consistently make money  playing this game you're supposed to  break even  it gets worse than that because you can  apply this strategy to any betting  situation no matter the odds you just  bet a little bit more than your total  losses so far but wait before you go and  bet on your house you can see that this  is clearly stupid for most betting  situations most of the time bets are  sacked well against you let's say you're  playing a game where you have a one in  ten chance of winning each bet which is  not at all unrealistic then the chance  that you'll even lose twelve games in a  row is still stupidly high at about  thirty percent but at that point you'll  have spent about eight thousand and  you're still likely to lose more so ya  don't actually go and do this in real  life please the strategy doesn't  actually work it's obvious why not when  the odds are like this but when the odds  are at fifty fifty like in the roulette  game it's hard to see what the problem  is and that's where the paradox is I'm  going to give you a week to see if you  can figure it out it took me a lot of  frustrated hours with a friend until we  cracked it but it was good fun to think  about which is why I wanted to tell you  guys it's a really interesting paradox  and if you do figure it out here are  some other variations that you might  want to try what if you decide not to be  too greedy and you only bet your total  losses plus one each time instead of two  or what if you get super greedy and you  bet your losses plus some growing amount  in these two scenarios see how quickly  the amount of money you bet grows and  what you expect your winnings to be if  you need some hints I have some sm